// The registry's own reads, and the four rollups the app's detail view folds
// into one answer (D6).
//
// ===========================================================================
// FOUR OF THESE SIX QUERIES READ A CONCEPT THIS DOMAIN DOES NOT OWN
// ===========================================================================
// `dsl/worker/queries.memql` is the precedent -- it declares
// `query plan runningPlansForUser` over `planner.concepts.plan` under a `use`
// -- and the reasoning is the same here. What makes a rollup a rollup is the
// ACCOUNT it is asked about; the four populations belong to four different
// domains, and putting each rollup beside its own concept would scatter one
// feature across four files whose only shared fact is a field name.
//
// EVERY ONE OF THEM READS THROUGH THE NORMAL AUTHORIZED PATH, and that is
// D1 restated as code: each filter is the tie-field equality ANDed with the
// concept's OWN tier predicate, exactly as the concept's own queries write
// it. Not one of them narrows a read BECAUSE of an account -- a caller sees
// precisely the sites, files, domains and invitations they could already see,
// arranged by who the work was for. An account is a record, so a rollup can
// only ever be a presentation of rows the engine had already decided to hand
// over.
//
// The consequence is worth stating plainly because a reader will wonder: two
// people can open the same account and see different rollups. That is
// correct. The account is shared; the rows are not.

use campaigns.concepts.{ campaign }
use campaigns.shapes.{ campaignFull }
use common.specs.{ requiresOwnerOrAdmin }
use common.traits.{ isNotArchived, isNotDeleted }
use identity.concepts.{ invitation }
use identity.shapes.{ invitationAdminSummary }
use knowledge.concepts.{ knowledgeDomain }
use knowledge.shapes.{ knowledgeDomainFull }
use library.concepts.{ artifact }
use library.shapes.{ artifactFull }
use platform.concepts.{ site }
use platform.shapes.{ siteFull }

/// Every account this caller may see -- their own, or every account in the
/// cluster when the caller is a cluster owner. The Accounts app's primary
/// screen, and the source every account picker in the OS reads.
///
/// ARCHIVED ROWS ARE EXCLUDED BY DEFAULT and returned under the filter (D8).
/// The archive term is a DISJUNCTION rather than a `when(args.includeArchived)`
/// guard, and the difference is not stylistic: `when(...)` drops its block on
/// the argument's ABSENCE, not on its falsity, so a caller passing
/// `includeArchived: false` -- which is exactly what a checkbox bound to a
/// boolean sends -- would have widened the read to every archived row. Written
/// as `isNotArchived || args.includeArchived==true` the three cases are the three answers:
/// absent resolves nil and fails the comparison (active only), false fails it
/// (active only), true admits everything.
///
/// "Everything", note, and not "the archived ones": a person looking for a
/// client they filed away wants it in its place in the list, marked, not in a
/// separate list of the forgotten.
///
/// The caller term is the composite tier's own predicate written out, which is
/// what TestRowAuthzEnforcementLandGate requires of an authored query over a
/// tier-declaring concept.
@actor
query account clientAccountsAll {
  args {
    includeArchived  boolean
  }
  filter  (ownerUserId==actor.userId || actor.isClusterOwner==true) && (isNotArchived || args.includeArchived==true)
  shape   clientAccountFull
  sort    "name", "asc"
}

/// Resolve one account by its own row id. The detail view's read on open, and
/// the read every tie surface uses to put a name on an id it holds.
@unbounded("account by id -- bounded to a single row (id is the row's own primary key)")
@actor
query account clientAccountById {
  args {
    accountId  string!
  }
  filter  row.id==args.accountId && (ownerUserId==actor.userId || actor.isClusterOwner==true)
  shape   clientAccountFull
}

/// The `self` singleton, if it has been materialized. The boot seed's existence
/// probe, and nothing else calls it.
///
/// IT CARRIES THE COMPOSITE TIER, AND THE AUTOMATION IS ELEVATED TO MATCH.
/// The first cut of this query omitted the tier conjunct, on the reasoning that
/// the caller is a boot automation with no signed-in person behind it. The
/// row-authz land gate refused it, and the gate was right: enforcement ANDs the
/// tier into every read of this concept whatever the filter says, so omitting
/// it does not make the read unscoped -- it makes the read SILENTLY narrowed,
/// and the `self` row is cluster-owned (ownerUserId empty), which the owned
/// branch can never match.
///
/// What that would have cost is worse than the two maintenance sweeps whose
/// entries sit beside this automation's in component/auth/maintenance_actor.go,
/// and worse in the opposite direction. A narrowed sweep skips work and looks
/// idle. A narrowed probe here reports the row ABSENT -- and absent means
/// CREATE, so the seed would re-run on every boot forever, which is precisely
/// the clobbering D3 forbids. `seedSelfAccount` is therefore on the maintenance
/// allowlist, which runs it as RoleOwner: the cluster-owner escape is the only
/// branch that can see this row, and the automation now has it.
///
/// @serverOnly is the other half. An ungated read projecting contact fields is
/// the `searchUsers` shape the PII gate exists to refuse (memql#2883), and the
/// app has no business calling this one -- it reads the same row through
/// `clientAccountById`, which carries the same tier and is on the wire.
///
/// WHY actor.userId SCOPING IS NOT THE FIX. There is no actor and there is no
/// owner. The caller is a boot automation, so no person is signed in for
/// `actor.userId` to name; and the row it looks for is CLUSTER-OWNED --
/// `ownerUserId` is empty by design, because the owner's own company is the
/// deployment's row rather than whichever principal happened to boot it, and
/// sameRowAuthzOwner refuses an empty owner outright. So a self-scoped filter
/// would match zero rows for the only caller this query will ever have, and
/// borrowing one user's authority (auth.ContextWithUserActor) would not help
/// either: there is no owner to borrow. The cluster-owner escape is the only
/// branch that can see this row, which is why the automation is on the
/// maintenance list rather than this query being scoped.
@actor
@serverOnly
@unbounded("the self account singleton -- bounded to a single row (literal id)")
query account existingSelfAccount {
  filter  row.id=="v1:accounts:account:self" && (ownerUserId==actor.userId || actor.isClusterOwner==true)
  shape   clientAccountFull
}

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The four rollups
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

/// The deployables tied to one account.
///
/// `isNotDeleted` and the composite-tier term are `sitesAll`'s own conjuncts,
/// repeated here rather than referenced: this is the same read that query
/// makes, narrowed by the tie field, and a soft-deleted site must stay gone
/// from a rollup for the reason it is gone from the list.
///
/// `isNotArchived` joined them when v1:platform:site gained the archived rung
/// (epic memql#4794, D10), and it is not optional for a ROLLUP: the band this
/// feeds says "and N more, in Deployables", so a count that included archived
/// sites would point the reader at a list that disagrees with it. A band is a
/// count of what is currently filed for a client, not of everything ever --
/// which is the same reading libraryItemsForAccount already takes.
@actor
query site sitesForAccount {
  args {
    accountId  string!
  }
  filter  accountId==args.accountId && isNotDeleted && isNotArchived && (ownerUserId==actor.userId || actor.isClusterOwner==true)
  shape   siteFull
  sort    "hostname", "asc"
}

/// The Library items labelled with one account.
///
/// `accountIds` is a LIST (D5 -- "one or two accounts" is the owner's own
/// framing), so the tie term is membership rather than equality.
///
/// ARCHIVED ROWS ARE EXCLUDED, matching `libraryArtifacts` rather than this
/// file's own `clientAccountsAll`: the account filter above is a view over the
/// registry a person is administering, while this is a count of what is
/// currently filed for a client. `archived!=true` rather than `==false`,
/// because a row promoted before the field existed carries no key at all and
/// `==false` would silently exclude it.
@actor
query artifact libraryItemsForAccount {
  args {
    accountId  string!
  }
  filter  args.accountId in accountIds && archived!=true && ownerUserId==actor.userId
  shape   artifactFull
  sort    "updatedAt", "desc"
}

/// The knowledge domains tagged with one account.
///
/// A TAG AND NOTHING MORE (D5). This read is the only consumer of
/// `v1:knowledge:knowledgeDomain.accountId` in the tree, and the field is
/// consulted by nothing in routing, attachment, retrieval or scoring. A domain
/// tagged with an account trains, attaches and answers exactly as an untagged
/// one does.
///
/// No tier conjunct, because the concept declares no tier -- see its
/// declaration in dsl/knowledge/concepts.memql for why that matches its
/// sibling `documentChunk` rather than departing from it.
query knowledgeDomain domainsForAccount {
  args {
    accountId  string!
  }
  filter  accountId==args.accountId
  shape   knowledgeDomainFull
  sort    "name", "asc"
}

/// The guest invitations sent on behalf of one account.
///
/// `invitationAdminSummary`, NEVER `invitationFull`, and this is the rule the
/// Users app's own review wrote (memql#4735): `invitationFull` projects
/// `tokenHash`, `previousTokenHash` and `bindingHash` -- not the plaintext
/// token, but the key the resolve path looks an invitation up BY -- and a
/// rollup that says how many guests a client has has never needed them.
///
/// `requiresOwnerOrAdmin` is `pendingUserInvitations`' own gate, carried here
/// for the same reason: the invitation concept declares no row tier, so the
/// spec IS the authorization on this read, and a rollup that dropped it would
/// be the shortest path in the product to reading every invitation in the
/// cluster. The Accounts app is not admin-gated, so below that floor this
/// section renders empty -- which is the engine's answer, rendered.
@actor
query invitation invitationsForAccount {
  args {
    accountId  string!
  }
  filter  accountId==args.accountId && requiresOwnerOrAdmin
  shape   invitationAdminSummary
  sort    "row.createdAt", "desc"
}

/// The email campaigns run for one account.
///
/// The composite-tier term is `campaigns`' own conjunct, repeated here rather than referenced, for
/// the reason `sitesForAccount` repeats `sitesAll`'s: this is the same read that query makes,
/// narrowed by the tie field, and a rollup that dropped the tier predicate would be the shortest
/// path in the product to reading every operator's campaigns.
///
/// It counts CAMPAIGNS and not sends. A band that said "4,182" would be counting delivery rows,
/// which is a number about one busy week rather than about the client -- and the delivery ledger is
/// deliberately not readable in bulk from here at all (memql#4823). Cancelled and failed campaigns
/// are included: a rollup is what is filed for this client, and a send that failed is still work
/// that happened.
@actor
query campaign campaignsForAccount {
  args {
    accountId  string!
  }
  filter  accountId==args.accountId && (ownerUserId==actor.userId || actor.isClusterOwner==true)
  shape   campaignFull
  sort    "row.createdAt", "desc"
}
